# DNA Splice-Junction Classification — Data Exploration

This notebook explores the UCI Molecular Biology Splice Junction dataset.

The goal is to understand the dataset structure, DNA sequences,
class distribution, and nucleotide composition before training
machine learning and deep learning models.

import pandas as pd
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import seaborn as sns

from src.preprocessing import (
    load_raw_data,
    build_sequences,
    clean_sequences,
    prepare_labels
)

# Load the dataset

X, y = load_raw_data()

print("Features shape:", X.shape)
print("Targets shape:", y.shape)

# Display the first five feature rows

X.head()

# Display the first five target rows

y.head()

# Display the feature column names

print(X.columns.tolist())

# Build DNA sequences

sequences = build_sequences(X)
sequences = clean_sequences(sequences)

print(sequences.head())

# Check sequence lengths

sequence_lengths = sequences.str.len()

print("Minimum sequence length:", sequence_lengths.min())
print("Maximum sequence length:", sequence_lengths.max())
print("Average sequence length:", sequence_lengths.mean())

# Prepare the class labels

labels, original_labels = prepare_labels(y)

print(original_labels.value_counts())

# Plot class distribution

plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))

sns.countplot(
    x=original_labels,
    order=original_labels.value_counts().index
)

plt.title("Splice-Junction Class Distribution")
plt.xlabel("Class")
plt.ylabel("Number of Sequences")

plt.show()

# Calculate nucleotide frequencies

nucleotide_counts = {
    "A": 0,
    "C": 0,
    "G": 0,
    "T": 0
}

for sequence in sequences:
    for base in sequence:
        if base in nucleotide_counts:
            nucleotide_counts[base] += 1

print(nucleotide_counts)

# Plot nucleotide frequencies

plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))

plt.bar(
    nucleotide_counts.keys(),
    nucleotide_counts.values()
)

plt.title("Nucleotide Frequencies")
plt.xlabel("Nucleotide")
plt.ylabel("Count")

plt.show()

## Observations

The dataset exploration above helps us understand:

- The number of DNA sequences available.
- The number of nucleotide features.
- The sequence length.
- The three splice-junction classes.
- The distribution of the classes.
- The nucleotide composition of the sequences.

These observations will guide the preprocessing and machine-learning
steps in the next notebooks.


